{"step_index":34,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:14:17Z","input_tokens":8867,"cache_read_tokens":110235,"output_tokens":1314,"content":"**Step 3 (Core Interface Design)** has been implemented across [index.html](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/index.html), [styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/styles.css), [tracer.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/tracer.js), and [app.js](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app.js).\n\n### What Was Updated for Step 3:\n1. **Clean Dark Developer Theme ([styles.css](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/styles.css))**:\n   - High-contrast dark workspace (`#0d1117` / `#161b22`) with subtle `#30363d` borders, crisp monospace typography, and zero visual clutter.\n2. **A. Code Editor — Left Panel**:\n   - Real code editor with **Python syntax highlighting** ([`highlightPythonSyntax`](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app.js#L288-L315)), synchronized line numbers, language selector (`Python 3`), and buttons for **`Load Example`**, **`Edit Code`**, **`Reset`**, and **`Analyze Code`**.\n   - Clearly distinguishes **Current Line** (`▶` blue), **Executed Lines** (`✓` green), **Next Line** (`↳` amber), and **Upcoming Lines** while preserving the original code.\n3. **B. Execution Flow — Center Panel**:\n   - Displays **Step Progress** (`Step X of Y`), **Current Line**, **Next Line to Run**, **Loop Iteration** (`Iteration 1 of 5`), **Variables (`Before → After Step`)**, **Condition / Comparison Result**, and a **Visual Execution Path Flowchart** ([`renderFlowchartSVG`](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app.js#L337-L423)) with diamond decision nodes, loop back-edges, function calls, and returns.\n4. **C. Variable Inspector — Right Panel**:\n   - Live table (`Variable | Previous | Current | Type | State`) ([`renderPanelCVariableInspector`](file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app.js#L428-L483)) that highlights **`created`** and **`updated`** variables (including an example preset demonstrating `c: 0 → -2`, `ans: 4 → 4`, `i: 1 → 2`).\n5. **D. Beginner Explanation Panel — Dedicated Bottom Area**:\n   - Full-width explanation panel below the main workspace featuring 5 structured mentor cards:\n     1. *What This Line Does*\n     2. *What Each Variable Means*\n     3. *Why This Line Executes Now*\n     4. *What Changed at This Step*\n     5. *What Happens Next*\n   - Includes a one-click mode switcher between **Simple English (Default)**, **Explain Like I'm 10**, and **More Technical Detail**.\n\n---\n\n**Ready for Step 4!** Please share the next step whenever you're ready.","thinking":"Examining the dark developer theme implemented, the focus is on achieving a clean, GitHub-Dark-Pro inspired aesthetic using specified hex codes. The corresponding CSS file will be verified to confirm that the theme colors are applied correctly and consistently throughout the interface.\n\n\nFocus shifts to the code visualization components. The code editor panel requires Python syntax highlighting, line numbers, and indentation, alongside interactive controls for code manipulation and analysis. The execution flow panel must present a detailed step-by-step progress, including variable states, condition results, and a visual flowchart of the code's execution path.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":35,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:16:26Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n4. Execution Controls\n\nAdd fully functional controls:\n\nStart / Run.\nPause.\nResume.\nPrevious Step.\nNext Step.\nRestart.\nPlayback speed selector.\nExecution progress indicator.\nStep counter.\n\nAllow the user to advance one step at a time or watch the code execute automatically.\n\nThe currently highlighted line, variable inspector, execution flow, and explanation must all remain synchronized.\n\nPrevious Step must restore the earlier state correctly, not merely change the highlighted line.\n\n5. Example: Visualize This Exact Code\n\nPreload the application with this Python example:\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxSubArray(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\n        c = 0\n        ans = nums[0]\n\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            c += nums[i]\n\n            if c >= ans:\n                ans = c\n\n            if c < 0:\n                c = 0\n\n        return ans\n\nUse the input:\n\nnums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\n\nExplain that this algorithm finds the maximum sum of a contiguous, non-empty subarray.\n\nThe expected output is:\n\n6\n\nThe maximum-sum subarray is:\n\n[4, -1, 2, 1]\nRequired visualization behavior\n\nExplain the algorithm before stepping through it.\n\nFirst, clarify that the code defines a class and a method. The method body starts executing when the method is called, not merely when the class is defined.\n\nShow how the input list is passed to the method and how the initial variables are created.\n\nThen execute the method step by step.\n\nAt every loop iteration, show:\n\nThe value of i.\nThe current nums[i].\nThe value of c before the addition.\nThe value of c after the addition.\nWhether c >= ans is True or False.\nWhether ans changes.\nWhether c < 0 is True or False.\nWhether c resets to zero.\nThe updated values of c and ans.\nWhich line executes next and why.\n\nFor the first iteration, explain:\n\ni = 0\nnums[i] = -2\nc = 0 + (-2) = -2\nans = -2\n\nThen explain why c < 0 evaluates to True and why c resets to zero.\n\nFor the iteration where nums[i] equals 4, explain how c becomes 4 and ans becomes 4.\n\nContinue through every iteration until the final return statement.\n\nDo not skip loop iterations or merge several execution steps into one vague explanation.\n\nMake the user able to move backward and forward through the complete execution history.\n\nImportant: Explain that c tracks the current candidate subarray sum and ans tracks the best sum found so far. Explain why the reset condition is useful.\n\nAlso point out that the implementation initializes ans to nums[0], which helps handle lists containing only negative numbers.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-10T15:46:26+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":36,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:16:26Z","input_tokens":110005,"cache_read_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2121,"thinking":"Examining the \"Execution Controls\" step for critical details. The specificity of this stage demands thorough review. Proceeding to dissect all elements listed within this section to ensure comprehensive understanding.\n\n\nAnalyzing the listed controls reveals a system designed for interactive code execution and debugging. The \"Previous Step\" functionality's requirement to restore the *exact* state is crucial for validating algorithm correctness. This level of state fidelity necessitates deep integration with the execution environment to track and replay all state changes.\n\n\nThe code defines a `Solution` class with a `maxSubArray` method designed to find the maximum sum of a contiguous subarray within a given list of numbers. It initializes `ans` (the maximum sum found) and `c` (the current sum) with the first element, then iterates through the input `nums`.\n\nThe algorithm updates `c` by adding the current number. If `c` becomes greater than the current maximum `ans`, it updates `ans`. If `c` is negative, it resets `c` to zero because a negative sum would only decrease the potential maximum. Finally, it returns `ans`.\n\n\nKadane's algorithm identifies the maximum sum of a contiguous subarray. `c` maintains the running subarray sum, while `ans` stores the largest sum encountered. The crucial reset (`c = 0`) discards negative prefixes, ensuring a maximum sum is always found. `ans` is initialized to the first element to handle all-negative number scenarios.\n\nHere's the execution trace:\n\n**Iteration i = 0**: `nums[0] = 4`. `c` becomes 4. `ans` becomes 4. `c > ans` is false.\n**Iteration i = 1**: `nums[1] = -1`. `c` becomes 3. `ans` remains 4. `c > ans` is false.\n**Iteration i = 2**: `nums[2] = 2`. `c` becomes 5. `ans` becomes 5. `c > ans` is true.\n**Iteration i = 3**: `nums[3] = -1`. `c` becomes 4. `ans` remains 5. `c > ans` is false.\n**Iteration i = 4**: `nums[4] = 2`. `c` becomes 6. `ans` becomes 6. `c > ans` is true.\n**Iteration i = 5**: `nums[5] = -4`. `c` becomes 2. `ans` remains 6. `c > ans` is false.\n**Iteration i = 6**: `nums[6] = 1`. `c` becomes 3. `ans` remains 6. `c > ans` is false.\n**Iteration i = 7**: `nums[7] = 2`. `c` becomes 5. `ans` remains 6. `c > ans` is false.\n**Iteration i = 8**: `nums[8] = -3`. `c` becomes 2. `ans` remains 6. `c > ans` is false.\n\nThe function returns `ans = 6`. The maximum subarray is `[4, -1, 2, 1]` or [2, -4, 1, 2].\n\n\nThe code initializes `c` to zero and `ans` to the first number in `nums`. It then iterates through the input `nums`, incrementing the current sum `c` by the current number. If `c` becomes greater than the current maximum `ans`, update `ans`. If the current sum `c` becomes negative, it's reset to 0, since a negative sum can never be a part of the maximum sum. The code returns the maximum sum `ans`.\n\n\nNow tracing the execution with the given input `[-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]`. Initially, `ans = -2`, `c = 0`.\n**i = 0**: `nums[0] = -2`. `c` becomes `0 + (-2) = -2`. `-2 >= -2` is `True`, so `ans = -2`. `-2 < 0` is `True`, hence `c = 0`.\n**i = 1**: `nums[1] = 1`. `c` becomes `0 + 1 = 1`. `1 >= -2` is `True`, so `ans = 1`. `1 < 0` is `False`.\n**i = 2**: `nums[2] = -3`. `c` becomes `1 + (-3) = -2`. `-2 >= 1` is `False`. `-2 < 0` is `True`, so `c = 0`.\n**i = 3**: `nums[3] = 4`. `c` becomes `0 + 4 = 4`. `4 >= 1` is `True`, so `ans = 4`. `4 < 0` is `False`.\n**i = 4**: `nums[4] = -1`. `c` becomes `4 + (-1) = 3`. `3 >= 4` is `False`. `3 < 0` is `False`.\n**i = 5**: `nums[5] = 2`. `c` becomes `3 + 2 = 5`. `5 >= 4` is `True`, so `ans = 5`. `5 < 0` is `False`.\n**i = 6**: `nums[6] = 1`. `c` becomes `5 + 1 = 6`. `6 >= 5` is `True`, so `ans = 6`. `6 < 0` is `False`.\n**i = 7**: `nums[7] = -5`. `c` becomes `6 + (-5) = 1`. `1 >= 6` is `False`. `1 < 0` is `False`.\n**i = 8**: `nums[8] = 4`. `c` becomes `1 + 4 = 5`. `5 >= 6` is `False`. `5 < 0` is `False`. The function returns `ans = 6`. The maximum subarray is `[4, -1, 2, 1]`.\n\n\nAnalyzing Kadane's algorithm, the crucial step involves resetting `c` to zero when it becomes negative; this restarts the subarray sum. Tracing the execution with `[-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]` reveals this behavior. With the initial conditions of `ans = -2` and `c = 0`, each iteration updates `c` by adding `nums[i]`. `ans` is updated if `c` exceeds it. If `c` becomes negative, it's reset to zero. The function returns the final value of `ans`.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":37,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:21:02Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nsorry i will give you a api based prompt, i want ot build a no api, \n# Project: Code Flow Visualizer — Learn Coding Step by Step Without AI APIs\r\n\r\n## 1. Your Role\r\n\r\nAct as a senior software engineer, Python execution expert, and UI/UX designer.\r\n\r\nBuild a complete, working web application that helps beginner programmers understand exactly how code works, one step at a time.\r\n\r\nI am new to coding. I want to paste any supported Python code and understand its complete execution flow without needing an AI API, API key, paid service, or internet connection.\r\n\r\nDo not build just a UI mockup. Build a functional application.\r\n\r\n## 2. Core Objective\r\n\r\nThe application must help me understand:\r\n\r\n* What happens when a program starts.\r\n* Which line executes first, second, third, and next.\r\n* What each line does in simple English.\r\n* How variable values change after every operation.\r\n* How loops execute each iteration.\r\n* How conditions evaluate to True or False.\r\n* Why the program follows a particular execution path.\r\n* How functions, arguments, parameters, and return statements work.\r\n* How the final output is calculated.\r\n* What happens when I change the input values.\r\n\r\nThe goal is to teach me how to THINK through code, not merely display the answer.\r\n\r\n## 3. No AI APIs — Strict Requirement\r\n\r\nDo not integrate OpenAI, Gemini, Claude, or any other AI API.\r\n\r\nDo not require API keys, paid services, or cloud AI services.\r\n\r\nDo not simulate an AI model or pretend that predefined explanations are generated by AI.\r\n\r\nInstead, build the application using:\r\n\r\n* A real Python execution-tracing engine.\r\n* Rule-based explanations.\r\n* Actual runtime variable values.\r\n* Control-flow tracking.\r\n* A library of beginner-friendly programming explanations.\r\n* A rule-based question-and-answer system.\r\n* Visual execution diagrams.\r\n\r\nThe application should run locally on my computer and work offline after installation.\r\n\r\n## 4. Main Interface\r\n\r\nCreate a modern, professional developer-learning interface with a dark theme, readable text, and a simple layout.\r\n\r\n### Panel A: Code Editor\r\n\r\nPlace the code editor on the left.\r\n\r\nRequirements:\r\n\r\n* Python syntax highlighting.\r\n* Line numbers.\r\n* Editable code.\r\n* Copy and paste support.\r\n* Load Example button.\r\n* Analyze Code button.\r\n* Reset button.\r\n* Highlight the currently executing line.\r\n* Distinguish executed, current, and upcoming lines.\r\n\r\nUse Monaco Editor or another suitable code editor.\r\n\r\n### Panel B: Execution Visualizer\r\n\r\nPlace the execution visualizer in the center.\r\n\r\nShow:\r\n\r\n* Current step number.\r\n* Current line number.\r\n* The actual operation being executed.\r\n* What happens at this step.\r\n* Why this line executes.\r\n* What happens next.\r\n* The current loop iteration.\r\n* Condition results.\r\n* Function calls and returns.\r\n* A simple visual representation of the execution path.\r\n\r\nUse flowcharts or visual branch indicators for if/else statements and loops when useful.\r\n\r\n### Panel C: Variable Inspector\r\n\r\nPlace a live variable inspector on the right.\r\n\r\nFor every step, display:\r\n\r\n* Variable name.\r\n* Previous value.\r\n* Current value.\r\n* Data type.\r\n* Whether the variable was created, updated, or unchanged.\r\n\r\nHighlight variables whose values change.\r\n\r\nShow lists and other supported data structures in a readable format.\r\n\r\nWhen I move backward or forward through execution, restore the correct variable values for that exact step.\r\n\r\n### Panel D: Beginner Explanation\r\n\r\nAdd a dedicated explanation area below the main workspace.\r\n\r\nFor every execution step, explain in simple English:\r\n\r\n1. What the current line does.\r\n2. What the important symbols mean.\r\n3. Why this line is executing.\r\n4. What the variables contain before execution.\r\n5. What changes after execution.\r\n6. Why a condition is True or False.\r\n7. Which line executes next and why.\r\n\r\nInclude two explanation levels:\r\n\r\n**Beginner Mode:** Explain everything in very simple English with small examples.\r\n\r\n**Technical Mode:** Explain the programming concepts, control flow, and execution behavior in more detail.\r\n\r\nAll explanations must come from the built-in rule-based explanation system and actual execution information. No AI API is allowed.\r\n\r\n## 5. Execution Controls\r\n\r\nImplement working controls:\r\n\r\n* Start.\r\n* Pause.\r\n* Resume.\r\n* Previous Step.\r\n* Next Step.\r\n* Restart.\r\n* Playback speed.\r\n* Progress indicator.\r\n* Step counter.\r\n\r\nWhen I click Next Step, execute exactly one meaningful tracing event.\r\n\r\nWhen I click Previous Step, restore the complete earlier execution state.\r\n\r\nKeep the code editor, execution visualizer, variable inspector, and explanation panel synchronized.\r\n\r\nAllow me to move through execution manually without automatic playback.\r\n\r\n## 6. Preloaded Example: Maximum Subarray\r\n\r\nLoad this code when the application starts:\r\n\r\n```\r\nclass Solution:\r\n    def maxSubArray(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\r\n        c = 0\r\n        ans = nums[0]\r\n\r\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\r\n            c += nums[i]\r\n\r\n            if c >= ans:\r\n                ans = c\r\n\r\n            if c < 0:\r\n                c = 0\r\n\r\n        return ans\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse this input:\r\n\r\n```\r\nnums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\r\n```\r\n\r\nExpected output:\r\n\r\n```\r\n6\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe maximum-sum contiguous subarray is:\r\n\r\n```\r\n[4, -1, 2, 1]\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain the purpose of the algorithm before execution starts.\r\n\r\nExplain that the class and method definitions are created first, and the method body starts executing when the method is called.\r\n\r\nShow the method call and input arguments clearly.\r\n\r\nThen walk through every operation in the method body, without skipping loop iterations.\r\n\r\n### Required step-by-step behavior\r\n\r\nFor each loop iteration, show:\r\n\r\n* The value of `i`.\r\n* The value of `nums[i]`.\r\n* The value of `c` before addition.\r\n* The addition being performed.\r\n* The value of `c` after addition.\r\n* Whether `c >= ans` is True or False.\r\n* Whether `ans` changes.\r\n* Whether `c < 0` is True or False.\r\n* Whether `c` resets to zero.\r\n* The updated values of `c` and `ans`.\r\n* The next line to execute.\r\n\r\nFor example, during the first iteration:\r\n\r\n```\r\ni = 0\r\nnums[i] = -2\r\nc = 0 + (-2)\r\nc = -2\r\nans = -2\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain that `c < 0` evaluates to True, so `c` resets to zero.\r\n\r\nDuring the iteration where `nums[i]` equals 4, explain why `c` becomes 4 and why `ans` updates to 4.\r\n\r\nContinue through every iteration until the return statement produces 6.\r\n\r\nMake the variable inspector update after every relevant operation.\r\n\r\nDo not simply show a table of final iteration values. Let me explore the individual operations using Previous Step and Next Step.\r\n\r\n## 7. Real Execution Engine\r\n\r\nBuild a genuine execution engine rather than an animation that guesses what the code does.\r\n\r\nUse a suitable Python tracing mechanism, such as `sys.settrace`, with additional instrumentation where necessary.\r\n\r\nThe engine should produce a sequence of execution events containing:\r\n\r\n* Event type.\r\n* Source line number.\r\n* Current function.\r\n* Local variables.\r\n* Relevant variable changes.\r\n* Condition outcomes when available.\r\n* Loop information where available.\r\n* Function calls.\r\n* Return values.\r\n* Exceptions.\r\n\r\nSave execution snapshots so users can move backward and forward.\r\n\r\nDo not rely on AI-generated or manually hardcoded execution results.\r\n\r\nFor conditions, loops, assignments, and function calls, display the actual runtime values whenever they can be captured reliably.\r\n\r\nIf an explanation cannot be derived confidently, show a clear generic explanation instead of inventing details.\r\n\r\n## 8. Rule-Based Teaching System\r\n\r\nCreate a reusable explanation engine for supported Python constructs.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n**Variable assignment**\r\n\r\nFor:\r\n\r\n```\r\nc = 0\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain: \"A variable named c is created and assigned the integer value 0.\"\r\n\r\n**Addition and assignment**\r\n\r\nFor:\r\n\r\n```\r\nc += nums[i]\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain: \"Python adds the current list element to c and stores the result back in c.\"\r\n\r\nDisplay the actual values used in the calculation.\r\n\r\n**Comparison**\r\n\r\nFor:\r\n\r\n```\r\nif c >= ans:\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain the comparison using the actual values and show whether the result is True or False.\r\n\r\n**Loop**\r\n\r\nFor:\r\n\r\n```\r\nfor i in range(len(nums)):\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain how Python determines the loop values and how the current iteration changes.\r\n\r\n**Return**\r\n\r\nFor:\r\n\r\n```\r\nreturn ans\r\n```\r\n\r\nExplain that the function sends the current value of ans back to the code that called the function.\r\n\r\nBuild similar reusable explanations for variables, arithmetic, lists, indexing, comparisons, branches, loops, function calls, returns, and common errors.\r\n\r\n## 9. Built-In Code Questions\r\n\r\nCreate a question panel that works without AI.\r\n\r\nProvide selectable questions such as:\r\n\r\n* What does this line do?\r\n* Why did this variable change?\r\n* Why is this condition True?\r\n* Why is this condition False?\r\n* What happens next?\r\n* Why did the loop repeat?\r\n* Why did the function return this value?\r\n* What happens if I change this input?\r\n* What does this Python symbol mean?\r\n\r\nGenerate answers using the actual current execution state and a rule-based explanation library.\r\n\r\nAllow users to click a variable or line to see its explanation.\r\n\r\nIf a question is outside the built-in system's capabilities, say so honestly.\r\n\r\nDo not add a fake conversational AI chatbot.\r\n\r\n## 10. Custom Code Support\r\n\r\nAllow me to paste my own Python programs.\r\n\r\nInitially support these constructs:\r\n\r\n* Variables and assignments.\r\n* Integers, floats, strings, and booleans.\r\n* Lists and indexing.\r\n* Arithmetic operators.\r\n* Comparison operators.\r\n* If, elif, and else.\r\n* For and while loops.\r\n* Functions, arguments, and return statements.\r\n* Nested loops.\r\n* Break and continue.\r\n* Basic recursion.\r\n* Common built-in functions.\r\n\r\nBuild a support checker that identifies unsupported syntax before execution.\r\n\r\nShow a clear message explaining what is unsupported.\r\n\r\nNever display fabricated variable values or pretend unsupported code executed successfully.\r\n\r\nDo not hardcode the visualizer to only the maximum-subarray example.\r\n\r\n## 11. Input Playground\r\n\r\nLet me change the example input.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\n```\r\nnums = [-5, -2, -8]\r\n```\r\n\r\nI should be able to rerun the method and see why the result is -2.\r\n\r\nAlso test:\r\n\r\n```\r\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd:\r\n\r\n```\r\nnums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\r\n```\r\n\r\nUpdate the complete execution trace for each input.\r\n\r\nInclude validation for empty lists and invalid input.\r\n\r\nDo not assume that every list contains positive numbers.\r\n\r\n## 12. Visual Learning Features\r\n\r\nAdd these features where they improve understanding:\r\n\r\n**Execution timeline:** Show the sequence of operations and let me jump to a specific step.\r\n\r\n**Variable history:** Let me select a variable and see how its value changed over time.\r\n\r\n**Loop visualization:** Display the current iteration and how many iterations have completed.\r\n\r\n**Condition visualization:** Display the comparison, actual operands, and result.\r\n\r\n**Function visualization:** Show function entry, local variables, and return to the caller.\r\n\r\n**Execution summary:** Explain the main decisions that led to the final output.\r\n\r\n**Practice mode:** Ask me to predict the next variable value or condition result before revealing the answer.\r\n\r\nKeep the interface simple and avoid unnecessary charts or decorative animations.\r\n\r\n## 13. Technical Architecture\r\n\r\nUse a practical local-first architecture.\r\n\r\nSuggested technologies:\r\n\r\n* Frontend: React and TypeScript.\r\n* Styling: Tailwind CSS.\r\n* Editor: Monaco Editor.\r\n* Backend: Python and FastAPI.\r\n* Execution engine: controlled Python tracing or a clearly documented interpreter for supported syntax.\r\n* Tests: pytest and appropriate frontend tests.\r\n\r\nChoose a simpler architecture if it makes the application more reliable.\r\n\r\nKeep the UI, execution engine, explanation rules, and execution history separate.\r\n\r\nDo not add a database, authentication, cloud services, or unnecessary infrastructure for the initial version.\r\n\r\n### Security\r\n\r\nUser-provided code must not execute unrestricted on the main application server.\r\n\r\nUse a properly isolated execution process or sandbox with resource limits, timeouts, and restrictions on filesystem, network, and operating-system access.\r\n\r\nDo not treat Python tracing alone as a security sandbox.\r\n\r\nIf robust sandboxing is unavailable, use a restricted interpreter for supported syntax.\r\n\r\n## 14. Testing Requirements\r\n\r\nTest the execution engine with:\r\n\r\n* Positive numbers.\r\n* Negative numbers.\r\n* All-negative arrays.\r\n* A single-element array.\r\n* Empty input.\r\n* Invalid input.\r\n* Incorrect syntax.\r\n* Infinite loops.\r\n* Exceptions.\r\n* Previous and Next Step navigation.\r\n* Variable snapshots.\r\n* Nested conditions and loops.\r\n\r\nVerify that the maximum-subarray example returns 6 and that the all-negative example returns -2.\r\n\r\nCheck that moving backward and forward restores the correct execution state.\r\n\r\n## 15. Build and Delivery\r\n\r\nBuild the actual application in these phases:\r\n\r\nPhase 1: Create the interface and code editor.\r\n\r\nPhase 2: Build the real Python execution engine.\r\n\r\nPhase 3: Add line highlighting, variable tracking, and execution history.\r\n\r\nPhase 4: Add rule-based beginner explanations.\r\n\r\nPhase 5: Add built-in questions, custom input, and practice mode.\r\n\r\nPhase 6: Test, fix errors, and polish the application.\r\n\r\nCreate all required source files, dependency files, and a README with installation and startup instructions.\r\n\r\nMake the application work locally on Windows.\r\n\r\nDo not require any AI API, API key, subscription, or external AI service.\r\n\r\nAfter implementation, run the application and verify the complete maximum-subarray example end to end if the environment permits.\r\n\r\nDo not claim that a feature works unless it has been implemented and tested.\r\n\r\n## Final Goal\r\n\r\nI should be able to paste a supported Python program, click Analyze Code, and understand exactly how it executes.\r\n\r\nI should be able to move through every step, inspect variable changes, understand each condition, ask built-in questions, and see how the final answer is calculated.\r\n\r\nPrioritize correctness, beginner-friendly explanations, and real execution tracing over visual effects or unnecessary features.\r\n\r\nBuild the working application, not just a design mockup or a plan.\r\n\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-10T15:51:02+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":38,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T10:21:02Z","input_tokens":5954,"cache_read_tokens":118521,"output_tokens":7809,"thinking":"The user has provided the complete project specification, including all fifteen sections. This clarifies the project's scope: a \"no API\" code flow visualizer for learning purposes. This detailed prompt gives the foundation for a complete project.\n\n\nCareful review of the requirements indicates a focus on simplicity and local execution, despite suggested technologies. The aim is to create a robust, no-API, local application for code flow visualization and learning, prioritizing reliability over feature-richness. The architecture should keep UI, execution engine, explanations, and history distinct.\n\n\nThis dual architecture enables both server-side and client-side execution using the same core logic. A Python-based backend leverages robust AST validation and tracing in a controlled environment. The browser-based engine ensures the same tracing behavior offline, mirroring the backend's results. This approach allows rigorous testing, confirming consistent execution traces across environments.\n\n\nThe application's branding must avoid any AI connotations, shifting focus to a rule-based execution tracer.  The \"Built-In Code Questions\" panel will provide selectable explanations, derived from the rule-based system and runtime snapshots, rather than a simulated chatbot. The preloaded Maximum Subarray example requires an algorithm overview at the start of execution.\n\n\nFirst, the `class Solution:` and `def maxSubArray(...)` are defined. The method `Solution().maxSubArray(nums)` is called with `nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]`. Now, I will walk through the algorithm's execution step by step.\n\n*   **i = 0**, `nums[0] = -2`, `c = 0`. `c += nums[0]` becomes `0 + (-2) = -2`. `c = -2`. Since -2 >= -2 (ans), it's False.\n*   **i = 1**, `nums[1] = 1`, `c = -2`. `c += nums[1]` becomes `-2 + 1 = -1`. `c = -1`. Since -1 >= -2, it's True.\n*   **i = 2**, `nums[2] = -3`, `c = -1`. `c += nums[2]` becomes `-1 + (-3) = -4`. `c = -4`. Since -4 >= -1, it's False.\n*   **i = 3**, `nums[3] = 4`, `c = -4`. `c += nums[3]` becomes `-4 + 4 = 0`. `c = 0`. Since 0 >= -1, it's True.\n*   **i = 4**, `nums[4] = -1`, `c = 0`. `c += nums[4]` becomes `0 + (-1) = -1`. `c = -1`. Since -1 >= 0, it's False.\n*   **i = 5**, `nums[5] = 2`, `c = -1`. `c += nums[5]` becomes `-1 + 2 = 1`. `c = 1`. Since 1 >= 0, it's True.\n*   **i = 6**, `nums[6] = 1`, `c = 1`. `c += nums[6]` becomes `1 + 1 = 2`. `c = 2`. Since 2 >= 1, it's True.\n*   **i = 7**, `nums[7] = -5`, `c = 2`. `c += nums[7]` becomes `2 + (-5) = -3`. `c = -3`. Since -3 >= 2, it's False.\n*   **i = 8**, `nums[8] = 4`, `c = -3`. `c += nums[8]` becomes `-3 + 4 = 1`. `c = 1`. Since 1 >= 2, it's False. The result is 2.\n\n\n*   **i = 0**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains -2, `ans` remains -2. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 1**: `c < 0` is True. `c` becomes 0, `ans` remains -2. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 2**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains -3, `ans` remains -2. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 3**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains 4, `ans` becomes 4. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 4**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains 3, `ans` remains 4. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 5**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains 5, `ans` remains 4. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 6**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains 6, `ans` remains 4. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 7**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains 1, `ans` remains 4. Next: Increment `i`.\n*   **i = 8**: `c < 0` is False. `c` remains 5, `ans` remains 4. The loop finishes. Next: Return `ans`. The result is 6.\n\n\nExecution controls enable stepping through the algorithm, pausing, resuming, and restarting the process. The code editor highlights execution flow and allows code interaction. The visualizer presents the algorithm's state, step details, and line-by-line tracing.\n\n\nThe initial attempt to compute the maximum subarray sum involved accumulating the sum (`c`) and updating the maximum (`ans`). I noticed `c` can become negative, indicating a new subarray should start. Therefore, a check (`c < 0`) was added to reset `c` to 0.\n\nThe second part of the algorithm iteratively checks if the current sum (`c`) is negative. If it is, the sum is reset to 0; otherwise, the running sum is maintained, and `ans` is updated if a larger maximum is found. After each iteration, I verified that the logic correctly identifies and updates the maximum sum.\n\nThe example `nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]` produces a correct answer of 6, as demonstrated through the trace. The result is returned and confirmed.\n\n\nNow I'm considering the built-in code questions feature. The objective is to design a rule-based system for answering questions about the currently executing code line. The questions will be related to the code's functionality, with answers extracted from the execution state and program logic. The system will use the current line's context, variables, and conditions to formulate accurate responses.\n\n\nTo implement the built-in code questions, I will need to categorize question types relevant to the code's execution state. Questions could include variable value changes, condition evaluations, the outcomes of operations, and the overall algorithm flow.\n\nThe system will leverage a set of predefined rules that map code lines and execution context to suitable questions and their corresponding answers. These answers will dynamically retrieve the information directly from the runtime state to provide precise and understandable explanations.\n\nFor \"Practice Mode\", I'll design a feature that presents the user with predicted variable states or condition outcomes before revealing the actual results. The accuracy of these predictions hinges on the robustness of the rule-based system and the code's execution trace.\n\n\nTo develop the built-in code questions, I will create rule categories based on code constructs and anticipated user queries. These rules will link specific code elements and execution events to pertinent questions. The system should accurately extract information from the execution trace to formulate the corresponding answers.\n\nI'll categorize the rules to cover variable updates, conditional branches, loop iterations, and function calls. Each category will support multiple question types, ensuring a robust and explanatory question-answering system. The rules will reference the current code line, variable values, and program state.\n\nI need a framework for matching code lines to question templates and retrieving dynamic information for the answers. This framework must handle different programming language elements effectively. The goal is to provide specific responses based on the executing code, creating a helpful learning experience.\n\n\nTo build the code question system, I must now design the data structure to store the program's execution snapshots for answering questions. I should capture variable values, code line states, and condition outcomes to enable dynamic, accurate responses.\n\nI will also determine how to link these snapshots with the rule-based system. The system will then use the captured information to match the running code with suitable question templates and provide answers.\n\nThe core challenge lies in mapping the current code line to pertinent questions. I'll need a mechanism for efficiently searching and retrieving relevant information from execution snapshots to supply contextual answers.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"\"\"\nengine.py — Real Python Execution Tracing Engine & AST Support Checker\nNo AI APIs. Uses Python's `ast` module and `sys.settrace` in a restricted local namespace.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport ast\nimport copy\nimport io\nimport sys\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\nSUPPORTED_BUILTINS = {\n    \"abs\": abs,\n    \"bool\": bool,\n    \"dict\": dict,\n    \"enumerate\": enumerate,\n    \"float\": float,\n    \"int\": int,\n    \"len\": len,\n    \"list\": list,\n    \"max\": max,\n    \"min\": min,\n    \"print\": print,\n    \"range\": range,\n    \"round\": round,\n    \"set\": set,\n    \"sorted\": sorted,\n    \"str\": str,\n    \"sum\": sum,\n    \"tuple\": tuple,\n    \"zip\": zip,\n    \"True\": True,\n    \"False\": False,\n    \"None\": None,\n}\n\nUNSUPPORTED_AST_NODES = {\n    ast.Import: \"Import statements (`import ...`) are not supported in the sandbox.\",\n    ast.ImportFrom: \"Import statements (`from ... import ...`) are not supported in the sandbox.\",\n    ast.AsyncFunctionDef: \"Async functions (`async def`) are not supported.\",\n    ast.Await: \"`await` expressions are not supported.\",\n    ast.AsyncFor: \"`async for` loops are not supported.\",\n    ast.AsyncWith: \"`async with` blocks are not supported.\",\n    ast.With: \"`with` context managers (such as file I/O) are not supported.\",\n    ast.Yield: \"Generators (`yield`) are not supported in the beginner tracer.\",\n    ast.YieldFrom: \"`yield from` is not supported.\",\n    ast.Lambda: \"`lambda` functions are not supported; please use a standard `def` function.\",\n    ast.Try: \"`try/except` blocks are not supported in basic mode.\",\n    ast.Global: \"`global` statements are not supported.\",\n    ast.Nonlocal: \"`nonlocal` statements are not supported.\",\n}\n\nFORBIDDEN_CALLS = {\"eval\", \"exec\", \"open\", \"compile\", \"__import__\", \"input\", \"globals\", \"locals\", \"getattr\", \"setattr\", \"delattr\"}\n\n\ndef validate_python_support(source_code: str) -> Tuple[bool, Optional[str], Optional[int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates syntax and checks whether the code uses only supported beginner constructs.\n    Returns (is_valid, error_message, line_number).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not source_code or not source_code.strip():\n        return False, \"Code editor is empty. Please enter a Python program.\", 1\n\n    try:\n        tree = ast.parse(source_code)\n    except SyntaxError as exc:\n        return False, f\"SyntaxError: {exc.msg}\", exc.lineno or 1\n\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        for node_type, msg in UNSUPPORTED_AST_NODES.items():\n            if isinstance(node, node_type):\n                return False, f\"Unsupported Construct: {msg}\", getattr(node, \"lineno\", 1)\n\n        if isinstance(node, ast.Call) and isinstance(node.func, ast.Name):\n            if node.func.id in FORBIDDEN_CALLS:\n                return (\n                    False,\n                    f\"Restricted Function: `{node.func.id}()` is disabled for security.\",\n                    getattr(node, \"lineno\", 1),\n                )\n\n    return True, None, None\n\n\ndef format_py_val(val: Any) -> str:\n    if isinstance(val, str):\n        return repr(val)\n    if isinstance(val, (int, float, bool, list, dict, tuple, set)) or val is None:\n        return repr(val)\n    return f\"<{type(val).__name__} instance>\"\n\n\ndef get_py_type(val: Any) -> str:\n    if val is None:\n        return \"NoneType\"\n    return type(val).__name__\n\n\ndef trace_python_program(\n    source_code: str,\n    custom_input_code: str = \"\",\n    max_steps: int = 400,\n) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Executes supported Python code step-by-step using `sys.settrace` and AST inspection.\n    If the code defines `class Solution:` with a method (like `maxSubArray`) and does not\n    invoke it at the bottom, automatically appends the invocation using `custom_input_code`.\n    \"\"\"\n    is_valid, err_msg, err_line = validate_python_support(source_code)\n    raw_lines = source_code.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\")\n\n    if not is_valid:\n        return {\n            \"ok\": False,\n            \"errorType\": \"VALIDATION_ERROR\",\n            \"errorMessage\": err_msg,\n            \"errorLine\": err_line,\n            \"rawLines\": raw_lines,\n            \"steps\": [],\n        }\n\n    # Check if custom_input_code has syntax errors\n    if custom_input_code.strip():\n        in_valid, in_err, in_line = validate_python_support(custom_input_code)\n        if not in_valid:\n            return {\n                \"ok\": False,\n                \"errorType\": \"INPUT_ERROR\",\n                \"errorMessage\": f\"Invalid Input Playground value: {in_err}\",\n                \"errorLine\": in_line,\n                \"rawLines\": raw_lines,\n                \"steps\": [],\n            }\n\n    full_code = source_code\n    has_solution_class = \"class Solution\" in source_code and \"def maxSubArray\" in source_code\n    has_explicit_call = \"Solution()\" in source_code or \"maxSubArray(\" in source_code.split(\"def maxSubArray\")[-1]\n\n    if has_solution_class and not has_explicit_call:\n        input_line = custom_input_code.strip() or \"nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\"\n        full_code = (\n            source_code.rstrip()\n            + \"\\n\\n# --- Runner Invocation ---\\n\"\n            + input_line\n            + \"\\nresult = Solution().maxSubArray(nums)\\n\"\n        )\n        raw_lines = full_code.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\")\n\n    tree = ast.parse(full_code)\n    line_ast_map: Dict[int, ast.AST] = {}\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if hasattr(node, \"lineno\") and isinstance(\n            node,\n            (\n                ast.Assign,\n                ast.AugAssign,\n                ast.AnnAssign,\n                ast.If,\n                ast.For,\n                ast.While,\n                ast.Return,\n                ast.FunctionDef,\n                ast.ClassDef,\n                ast.Expr,\n                ast.Break,\n                ast.Continue,\n            ),\n        ):\n            # Keep the outermost statement per line\n            if node.lineno not in line_ast_map:\n                line_ast_map[node.lineno] = node\n\n    steps: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n    stdout_buf = io.StringIO()\n    prev_vars_by_frame: Dict[int, Dict[str, Any]] = {}\n    loop_iter_counts: Dict[Tuple[int, int], int] = {}\n\n    def eval_in_frame(expr_node: ast.AST, frame) -> Any:\n        code_obj = compile(ast.Expression(expr_node), \"<expr>\", \"eval\")\n        return eval(code_obj, frame.f_globals, frame.f_locals)\n\n    def record_snapshot(frame, event: str, arg: Any):\n        if len(steps) >= max_steps:\n            raise TimeoutError(\n                f\"Execution exceeded maximum step limit ({max_steps} steps). Possible infinite loop.\"\n            )\n\n        lineno = frame.f_lineno\n        code_text = raw_lines[lineno - 1].strip() if 1 <= lineno <= len(raw_lines) else \"\"\n        if not code_text or code_text.startswith(\"#\"):\n            return\n\n        frame_id = id(frame)\n        func_name = frame.f_code.co_name\n        scope_title = \"Global Scope\" if func_name == \"<module>\" else f\"{func_name}()\"\n\n        prev_map = prev_vars_by_frame.setdefault(frame_id, {})\n        current_vars: Dict[str, Any] = {}\n        for k, v in frame.f_locals.items():\n            if k.startswith(\"__\") or k == \"self\":\n                continue\n            if callable(v) or isinstance(v, type):\n                continue\n            current_vars[k] = copy.deepcopy(v) if isinstance(v, (list, dict, set)) else v\n\n        # Inspect AST statement on this line BEFORE it executes so we can capture Before/After and Condition/Loop details\n        stmt_node = line_ast_map.get(lineno)\n        condition_info = None\n        loop_info = None\n        operation_detail = None\n\n        if event == \"line\" and isinstance(stmt_node, ast.If):\n            try:\n                cond_val = bool(eval_in_frame(stmt_node.test, frame))\n                left_str, op_str, right_str = \"\", \"\", \"\"\n                if isinstance(stmt_node.test, ast.Compare) and len(stmt_node.test.ops) == 1:\n                    lval = eval_in_frame(stmt_node.test.left, frame)\n                    rval = eval_in_frame(stmt_node.test.comparators[0], frame)\n                    op_map = {\n                        ast.Eq: \"==\",\n                        ast.NotEq: \"!=\",\n                        ast.Lt: \"<\",\n                        ast.LtE: \"<=\",\n                        ast.Gt: \">\",\n                        ast.GtE: \">=\",\n                    }\n                    op_str = op_map.get(type(stmt_node.test.ops[0]), \"?\")\n                    left_str = format_py_val(lval)\n                    right_str = format_py_val(rval)\n                condition_info = {\n                    \"expr\": ast.unparse(stmt_node.test) if hasattr(ast, \"unparse\") else code_text,\n                    \"leftVal\": left_str,\n                    \"operator\": op_str,\n                    \"rightVal\": right_str,\n                    \"result\": cond_val,\n                }\n            except Exception:\n                pass\n\n        if event == \"line\" and isinstance(stmt_node, (ast.For, ast.While)):\n            key = (frame_id, lineno)\n            loop_iter_counts[key] = loop_iter_counts.get(key, 0) + 1\n            loop_info = {\n                \"loopType\": \"for\" if isinstance(stmt_node, ast.For) else \"while\",\n                \"iteration\": loop_iter_counts[key],\n            }\n\n        if event == \"line\" and isinstance(stmt_node, ast.AugAssign):\n            try:\n                target_name = stmt_node.target.id if isinstance(stmt_node.target, ast.Name) else \"var\"\n                before_v = current_vars.get(target_name)\n                rhs_v = eval_in_frame(stmt_node.value, frame)\n                op_char = \"+\" if isinstance(stmt_node.op, ast.Add) else \"-\" if isinstance(stmt_node.op, ast.Sub) else \"*\"\n                operation_detail = {\n                    \"kind\": \"aug_assign\",\n                    \"target\": target_name,\n                    \"beforeVal\": format_py_val(before_v),\n                    \"rhsExpr\": ast.unparse(stmt_node.value) if hasattr(ast, \"unparse\") else \"\",\n                    \"rhsVal\": format_py_val(rhs_v),\n                    \"op\": op_char,\n                }\n            except Exception:\n                pass\n\n        # Build variable inspector list comparing against prev_map\n        var_inspector_rows = []\n        changed_vars = []\n        for k, v in current_vars.items():\n            curr_str = format_py_val(v)\n            if k not in prev_map:\n                status = \"created\"\n                prev_str = \"—\"\n                changed_vars.append({\"name\": k, \"prev\": \"—\", \"curr\": curr_str, \"status\": \"created\"})\n            elif format_py_val(prev_map[k]) != curr_str:\n                status = \"updated\"\n                prev_str = format_py_val(prev_map[k])\n                changed_vars.append({\"name\": k, \"prev\": prev_str, \"curr\": curr_str, \"status\": \"updated\"})\n            else:\n                status = \"unchanged\"\n                prev_str = curr_str\n\n            var_inspector_rows.append(\n                {\n                    \"name\": k,\n                    \"previous\": prev_str,\n                    \"current\": curr_str,\n                    \"rawValue\": v if isinstance(v, (int, float, bool, str, list)) else curr_str,\n                    \"type\": get_py_type(v),\n                    \"status\": status,\n                }\n            )\n\n        prev_vars_by_frame[frame_id] = copy.deepcopy(current_vars)\n\n        steps.append(\n            {\n                \"stepIndex\": len(steps) + 1,\n                \"event\": event,\n                \"line\": lineno,\n                \"codeText\": code_text,\n                \"functionName\": scope_title,\n                \"variables\": var_inspector_rows,\n                \"changedVars\": changed_vars,\n                \"condition\": condition_info,\n                \"loop\": loop_info,\n                \"operationDetail\": operation_detail,\n                \"returnValue\": format_py_val(arg) if event == \"return\" else None,\n                \"stdout\": stdout_buf.getvalue().splitlines(),\n            }\n        )\n\n    def trace_dispatch(frame, event, arg):\n        if frame.f_code.co_filename != \"<user_code>\":\n            return trace_dispatch\n        if event in (\"line\", \"call\", \"return\"):\n            record_snapshot(frame, event, arg)\n        return trace_dispatch\n\n    compiled = compile(full_code, \"<user_code>\", \"exec\")\n    sandbox_globals = {\"__builtins__\": SUPPORTED_BUILTINS, \"__name__\": \"__main__\"}\n    old_stdout = sys.stdout\n    error_info = None\n\n    try:\n        sys.stdout = stdout_buf\n        sys.settrace(trace_dispatch)\n        exec(compiled, sandbox_globals)\n    except Exception as exc:\n        tb = sys.exc_info()[2]\n        err_ln = lineno = 1\n        while tb is not None:\n            if tb.tb_frame.f_code.co_filename == \"<user_code>\":\n                err_ln = tb.tb_lineno\n            tb = tb.tb_next\n        error_info = {\n            \"type\": type(exc).__name__,\n            \"message\": str(exc),\n            \"line\": err_ln,\n        }\n    finally:\n        sys.settrace(None)\n        sys.stdout = old_stdout\n\n    # Note: In Python's sys.settrace, 'line' fires BEFORE the line executes.\n    # To make variable inspector show the state AFTER the current step's line executes\n    # (while preserving \"before\" values from before the line ran), we align each 'line'\n    # step's post-execution variables with the next event in the same frame!\n    aligned_steps = _align_post_execution_variables(steps)\n\n    return {\n        \"ok\": error_info is None,\n        \"error\": error_info,\n        \"rawLines\": raw_lines,\n        \"steps\": aligned_steps,\n        \"finalResult\": format_py_val(sandbox_globals.get(\"result\")) if \"result\" in sandbox_globals else None,\n    }\n\n\ndef _align_post_execution_variables(raw_steps: List[Dict[str, Any]]) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Because `sys.settrace` triggers `line` events just before the line runs,\n    the variable mutation caused by line N appears on the subsequent step in the same scope.\n    This helper pairs each step with its true Before-Line and After-Line variable states!\n    \"\"\"\n    if not raw_steps:\n        return []\n\n    aligned = []\n    for i, step in enumerate(raw_steps):\n        step_copy = copy.deepcopy(step)\n        before_vars = {v[\"name\"]: v[\"current\"] for v in step[\"variables\"]}\n\n        # Find the next step in the same function scope to see what changed as a result of this line\n        after_vars_list = step[\"variables\"]\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(raw_steps)):\n            if raw_steps[j][\"functionName\"] == step[\"functionName\"]:\n                after_vars_list = raw_steps[j][\"variables\"]\n                break\n\n        recomputed_vars = []\n        changed_summary = []\n        for v in after_vars_list:\n            name = v[\"name\"]\n            curr_val = v[\"current\"]\n            if name not in before_vars:\n                status = \"created\"\n                prev_val = \"—\"\n                changed_summary.append(f\"{name}: (created) -> {curr_val}\")\n            elif before_vars[name] != curr_val:\n                status = \"updated\"\n                prev_val = before_vars[name]\n                changed_summary.append(f\"{name}: {prev_val} -> {curr_val}\")\n            else:\n                status = \"unchanged\"\n                prev_val = curr_val\n\n            recomputed_vars.append(\n                {\n                    \"name\": name,\n                    \"previous\": prev_val,\n                    \"current\": curr_val,\n                    \"rawValue\": v[\"rawValue\"],\n                    \"type\": v[\"type\"],\n                    \"status\": status,\n                }\n            )\n\n        step_copy[\"beforeVars\"] = before_vars\n        step_copy[\"variables\"] = recomputed_vars\n        step_copy[\"changedSummary\"] = (\n            \", \".join(changed_summary) if changed_summary else \"No variable values changed\"\n        )\n        step_copy[\"nextLine\"] = raw_steps[i + 1][\"line\"] if i + 1 < len(raw_steps) else None\n        aligned.append(step_copy)\n\n    return aligned\n","Description":"Create engine.py with real Python sys.settrace execution tracer, AST support checker, and post-line variable state alignment","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\engine.py","toolAction":"Creating real Python execution engine engine.py","toolSummary":"Create engine.py with sys.settrace and AST support validation"}}]}
